The court may consider any of the following factors when determining if an interference with privacy is serious:
(a) the particular kind or kinds of information involved in the interference with privacy;
(b) the sensitivity of the personal information of the individual;
(c) the consequences, or potential consequences, of the interference with privacy for the individual;
(d) the number of individuals affected by the interference with privacy;
(e) whether the individual affected by the interference with privacy is a child or person experiencing vulnerability.

The condition that removes the prohibition on disclosure is when:

- The credit reporting body holds credit reporting information about the individual;
- The individual believes on reasonable grounds that the individual has been, or is likely to be, a victim of fraud (including identity fraud);
- The individual requests the body not to use or disclose the information under this Division.

This condition allows the credit provider to disclose the credit information during the ban period if these specific conditions are met.

According to s 30 of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), if the Commissioner reports to the Minister about conduct carried out under a practice after starting an investigation independently:

(a) The report shall set out in the report his or her findings and the reasons for those findings;

(b) It may include any recommendations by the Commissioner for preventing a repetition of the act or a continuation of the practice; 

(c) It may also include any recommendation by the Commissioner for either or both of the following:
   - The payment of compensation to a person who has suffered loss or damage as a result of the act or practice;
   - The taking of other action to remedy or reduce loss or damage suffered by a person as a result of the act or practice;

(d) It shall serve a copy of the report on the agency, file number recipient, credit reporting body or credit provider concerned and the Minister (if any) responsible for the agency, recipient, credit reporting body or credit provider; 

(e) It may also serve a copy of the report on any person affected by the act or practice.

The Commissioner must cover these elements in the report to fulfill the requirements set out under s 30.

The Commissioner may direct an agency to give a privacy impact assessment if:
(a) the activity or function might have a significant impact on the privacy of individuals;
(b) the Commissioner considers that the activity or function involves handling personal information about individuals. [Source: s 33D(1)]

After the Commissioner passes a complaint on, the following notification must be provided to the person who lodged it:

- The complaint may be continued as a complaint by the complainant on his or her own behalf against the respondent.
- The Commissioner may only make such a determination if the Commissioner is satisfied that it is in the interests of justice to do so for any of the following reasons:
  - the costs that would be incurred if the complaint were to continue as a representative complaint are likely to exceed the costs that would be incurred if each class member lodged a separate complaint;
  - the representative complaint will not provide an efficient and effective means of dealing with the complaints of the class members;
  - the complaint was not brought in good faith as a representative complaint;
  - it is otherwise inappropriate that the complaints be pursued by means of a representative complaint.

The Commissioner must also notify the complainant if there is no reasonable likelihood that the complaint will be resolved by conciliation.

After transfer, as a complaint made:

- To the Australian Human Rights Commission under Division 3 of Part II of the Australian Human Rights Commission Act 1986;
- To the National Data Commissioner under Part 5.3 of the Data Availability and Transparency Act 2022;
- To the Ombudsman under the Ombudsman Act 1976;
- To the Postal Industry Ombudsman under the Ombudsman Act 1976;
- To the Overseas Students Ombudsman under the Ombudsman Act 1976;
- To the National Student Ombudsman under the Ombudsman Act 1976;
- To the Inspector-General of Intelligence and Security under the Inspector-General of Intelligence and Security Act 1986; or

[Source: s 50 Reference of matters to other authorities]

The agency must take all such steps as are reasonably within his or her power to ensure that:
(a) the terms of the determination are brought to the notice of all members, officers and employees of the agency whose duties are such that they may engage in conduct of the kind to which the determination relates;
(b) no member, officer or employee of the agency repeats or continues conduct that is covered by a declaration included in the determination under subparagraph 52(1)(b)(i) or paragraph 52(1A)(a);
(c) the steps specified in a declaration included in the determination under subparagraph 52(1)(b)(ia) or paragraph 52(1A)(b) are taken within the specified period; and
(d) any act or course of conduct that is covered by a declaration included in the determination under subparagraph 52(1)(b)(ii) or paragraph 52(1A)(c) is performed.

According to s 62(1) and (3), the following persons may commence proceedings in the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) for an order to enforce a determination:

- If the determination was made under subsection 52(1)—the complainant;
- The Commissioner.

According to section 6G of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth), for an organisation or small business operator:

- Acting in the capacity of a real estate agent;
- Acting in the capacity of a general insurer within the meaning of the Insurance Act 1973;
- Acting in the capacity of an employer of an individual.

These roles mean that such operators are not treated as credit providers while performing them.

Even where information concerns a person's bankruptcy, it fails to be personal insolvency information if it concerns:

- The presentation of a creditor's petition against the individual;
- An administration under Part XI of the Bankruptcy Act of the individual's estate.

The grounds for which an Attorney-General may furnish a certificate under s 70(1) include:

(a) prejudice the security, defence or international relations of Australia;
(b) involve the disclosure of communications between a Minister of the Commonwealth and a Minister of a State, being a disclosure that would prejudice relations between the Commonwealth Government and the Government of a State;
(c) involve the disclosure of deliberations or decisions of the Cabinet or of a Committee of the Cabinet;
(d) involve the disclosure of deliberations or advice of the Executive Council;
(e) prejudice the conduct of an investigation or inquiry into crime or criminal activity that is currently being pursued, or prejudice the fair trial of any person;
(f) disclose, or enable a person to ascertain, the existence or identity of a confidential source of information in relation to the enforcement of the criminal law.

The Attorney-General may also furnish a certificate if they believe giving the information would be contrary to the public interest because it would:

(g) prejudice the effectiveness of the operational methods or investigative practices or techniques of agencies responsible for the enforcement of the criminal law; or
(h) endanger the life or physical safety of any person.

The Commissioner must not apply for an order under Part 4 of the Regulatory Powers Act in relation to a contravention of subsection 13G(1), 13H(1) or 13K(1) or (2) of this Act constituted by particular conduct engaged in by an entity, if:

- The entity has been given a notice under subsection (1) of this section in relation to a contravention constituted by the same conduct;
- Either of the following subparagraphs applies:
  - The notice has not been withdrawn, and the entity has complied with the notice;
  - The entity has made an application under section 80UD of this Act in relation to the notice and the application has not been completely dealt with.

This is based on s 80UC(7) of the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth).

According to section 1.4 of the Privacy Act, an APP entity's APP privacy policy must contain the following information:

(a) The kinds of personal information that the entity collects and holds;
(b) How the entity collects and holds personal information;
(c) The purposes for which the entity collects, holds, uses and discloses personal information;

The Immigration Department must reasonably believe that:
(i) if the entity is the Immigration Department—the collection of the information is reasonably necessary for, or directly related to, one or more enforcement related activities conducted by, or on behalf of, the entity;
(ii) otherwise—the collection of the information is reasonably necessary for, or directly related to, one or more of the entity's functions or activities.

Unless an exception is available (as per subclause 9.3 of Australian Privacy Principle 9), an organization must not use or disclose a government related identifier of an individual unless:

(a) the use or disclosure of the identifier is reasonably necessary for the organization to verify the identity of the individual for the purposes of the organization's activities or functions;

(b) the use or disclosure of the identifier is reasonably necessary for the organization to fulfill its obligations to an agency or a State/Territory authority; 

(c) the use or disclosure of the identifier is required or authorised by or under Australian law or a court/tribunal order;

(d) a permitted general situation (other than the situation referred to in items 4 or 5 of the table in subsection 16A(1)) exists in relation to the use or disclosure of the identifier; 

(e) the organization reasonably believes that the use or disclosure of the identifier is reasonably necessary for one or more enforcement related activities conducted by, or on behalf of, an enforcement body;

(f) subclause 9.3 applies in relation to the use or disclosure.

The exceptions include:

- Adoption of government related identifiers as its own identifiers under certain conditions
- Use or disclosure required or authorised by Australian law or a court
